{"word": "mermaid", "source": "freedictionaryapi", "data": {"word": "mermaid", "entries": [{"language": {"code": "en", "name": "English"}, "partOfSpeech": "noun", "pronunciations": [{"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈmɜːˌmeɪd/", "tags": ["UK"]}, {"type": "ipa", "text": "/ˈmɝ.meɪd/", "tags": ["US"]}], "forms": [{"word": "mermaids", "tags": ["plural"]}], "senses": [{"definition": "A mythological creature with a woman's head and upper body, and a tail of a fish.", "tags": [], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "Search the depth, & ſee that variety of Sea monſters & fiſhes, Mare-maids, Sea men, Horſes, &c. which it affords.", "reference": "1624, Democritus Junior [pseudonym; Robert Burton], The Anatomy of Melancholy: […], 2nd edition, Oxford, Oxfordshire: […] John Lichfield and James Short, for Henry Cripps, →OCLC, partition 2, section 2, member 3, page 210:"}, {"text": "People that are drowned, and whose bodies are not founds are believed to have been taken into the dwellings of the Mermaids.", "reference": "1828, Thomas Keightley, The Fairy Mythology, volume I, London: William Harrison Ainsworth, page 242:"}], "synonyms": ["mergirl", "merlady", "mermaiden", "merwoman"], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(as a modifier) Coloured a brilliant turquoise.", "tags": [], "examples": ["a mermaid smoothie", "mermaid ice-cream"], "quotes": [], "synonyms": [], "antonyms": [], "translations": [], "subsenses": []}, {"definition": "(obsolete) A prostitute.", "tags": ["obsolete"], "examples": [], "quotes": [{"text": "A Gentleman or an honeſt Cittizen, ſhall not Sit in your pennie-bench Theaters, vvith his Squirrell by his ſide cracking nuttes; nor ſneake into a Tauerne vvith his Mermaid; but he ſhall be Satyr'd and Epigram'd vpon, and his humour muſt run vpo'th Stage: […]", "reference": "1601 (first performance), Thomas Dekker, Satiro-mastix. Or The Untrussing of the Humorous Poet. […], London: […] [Edward Allde] for Edward White, […], published 1602, →O